From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: record that H.265 has no rotate field in hardware

cedrus_rotation() has kept rotation to H.264 on the strength of a comment in
the vendor decoder -- "Rotation doesn't support", with the degree hardcoded to
zero.  That is an argument from caution: the field is right there in
VE_DEC_H265_SDRT_CTRL[2:0], mainline has a macro for it, and nothing said
whether the silicon implements it.

It does not.  Writing ~0 to VE_DEC_H265_SDRT_CTRL reads back 0x00003f00, so
the only implemented bits are the scale shifts at [11:8] and the interlaced
controls at [13:12].  Write 7 to the rotate field and it reads back 0.

Behaviour agrees.  Requesting 90, 180 or 270 on an H.265 stream decodes
cleanly -- no timeout, no fault -- and emits an unrotated picture: a
four-quadrant card came back in its original orientation at every angle, while
the same card rotates correctly under H.264 at the same geometry.

No functional change.  This only replaces an inference with the measurement,
so the next person to notice the unused macro does not have to repeat the
experiment, and so the gate is understood as a hardware fact rather than a
policy that might be relaxed.

Origin: https://github.com/cstenger/allwinner-h713-mainline/blob/43b2cdd4aa1b161a281db8b10f02ce70085e2d42/patches/kernel/0117-media-cedrus-record-that-h265-has-no-rotate-field.patch (0117-media-cedrus-record-that-h265-has-no-rotate-field.patch, commit c59d89a)
---
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
@@ -289,9 +289,16 @@
  * s_ctrl instead broke the control framework's own range probing.
  */
 /*
- * Both engines scale by powers of two.  ROTATION stays H.264 only: the H.265
- * field exists but the vendor decoder comments it out with "Rotation doesn't
- * support" and ships zero, so it has no reference behaviour.
+ * Both engines scale by powers of two.  ROTATION stays H.264 only, and that is
+ * a property of the silicon rather than a cautious choice: the rotate field
+ * the H.265 engine appears to have at VE_DEC_H265_SDRT_CTRL[2:0] IS NOT
+ * IMPLEMENTED.  Writing ~0 to that register reads back 0x00003f00, so only the
+ * scale shifts at [11:8] and the interlaced controls at [13:12] exist; write 7
+ * to the rotate field and it reads back 0.  Requesting 90, 180 or 270 on an
+ * H.265 stream decodes cleanly and emits an UNROTATED picture -- measured with
+ * a four-quadrant card, which came back in its original orientation at every
+ * angle while the same test rotates correctly under H.264.  The vendor's
+ * "Rotation doesn't support" comment is literally true of the hardware.
  */
 static inline bool cedrus_can_scale(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx)
 {
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_regs.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_regs.h
@@ -492,6 +492,11 @@
 #define VE_DEC_H265_SDRT_CTRL			(VE_ENGINE_DEC_H265 + 0x50)
 /* Layout from the vendor's regHEVC_EXTRA_CTRL struct; [12]/[13] are
  * interlaced field controls and stay clear for progressive.
+ *
+ * The implemented bits are [13:8] ONLY -- a write of ~0 reads back 0x00003f00.
+ * ROTATE below therefore describes a field that does not exist on this part:
+ * it reads back 0 however it is written, and the picture comes out unrotated.
+ * It is kept to document the vendor layout, not because it can be used.
  */
 #define VE_DEC_H265_SDRT_CTRL_ROTATE(r)		SHIFT_AND_MASK_BITS((r) / 90, 2, 0)
 #define VE_DEC_H265_SDRT_CTRL_H_SHIFT(s)	SHIFT_AND_MASK_BITS(s, 9, 8)
